course: everyday
lesson: 442
title: Politicians and promises, as the news reports them
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Before an election, a candidate in Liam's town promised «a new swimming pool for every school». She won, and no pool was built. Liam's dad said she'd said the money had run out. Some neighbours said «She lied, plain and simple»; others said «She tried, the plans got blocked». Dad didn't tell Liam which side was right. He said: «You can check three things: what she promised, what she did, and what she says happened. Then make your own mind up.»
explain: The news can be confusing. One evening in Auckland, Ella heard two politicians say opposite things about crime: one said it went up, the other said it went down. Her teacher showed both charts: crime had gone down over ten years but up last year. Each politician picked the part that suited them. Noah's grandma watched a politician get a yes-or-no question and talk for two minutes about something else. «He didn't answer that, did he?» she said. Dodging a question isn't the same as lying — but it can hide things.
explain: Some tricks come from newspapers and the internet, not from politicians. A headline shouted «MAYOR'S SHOCKING SECRET!» Zara read the article: he likes pineapple on pizza. A video online showed a politician «saying» something terrible; Leo's uncle shared it, wicked angry («wicked» in Boston means very). Leo's sister noticed the mouth moved weirdly: it was a fake made by a computer. They checked — no real news site had reported it — and the uncle deleted it, a bit red-faced.
explain: Promises are worth checking. Max in Sydney promised «no homework ever» if he became class captain. He won, homework continued — a captain has no power over homework. A leaflet through Aisha's door said a candidate «will lower taxes for everyone and spend more on every school and hospital». Aisha's dad said «Both? With what money?» Then he added that some people think it can be done with clever choices, others think it can't, and they'd see. One politician said «I never said that!» — and a reporter played a recording where he did. People argued about whether he lied or forgot.
explain: In Belfast two neighbours argued about one politician. «He's a liar, everyone knows it.» «Wise up, he's the only honest one» («wise up» means stop being silly). Callum's mum said, «Let's see what he actually said and did, and decide ourselves.» Grace's dad in Toronto said «Politicians are all liars, eh.» Her teacher said, «Some lie, some don't, most do a mix. The important skill is checking.» Checking beats both trusting everyone and trusting no-one.
fact: A promise made before an election can be checked afterwards: what was promised, what was done, and what the person says happened.
fact: When a promise is broken, people often disagree about whether it was a lie or a plan that failed; both happen.
fact: Two people can use the same numbers to say opposite things by choosing different years or different parts.
fact: Answering a different question from the one asked is called dodging; it is not always lying, but it can hide things.
fact: Headlines are written to make people read; the story underneath may be much less exciting.
fact: Computers can make fake videos of real people saying things they never said.
fact: If something big is true, many trusted news sites report it; if only one strange account has it, be careful.
fact: Promises that need powers the person doesn't have, like a class captain cancelling homework, cannot be kept.
fact: When a promise gives everyone everything with nothing taken away, it is fair to ask how it would be paid for; honest people disagree about the answer.
fact: What politicians say in public is often recorded, so «I never said that» can be checked.
fact: «Everyone knows» is not proof; a fair judgement looks at what someone actually said and did.
fact: «All politicians are liars» is an exaggeration; some lie, some don't, and many do a mix.
fact: «Wicked» (New England, US) means very; «wise up» (Ireland, Northern Ireland) means stop being silly; «eh» (Canada) asks you to agree.
exercise: What three things did Liam's dad say you can check about a promise? | What was promised, what was done, and what she says happened | Her age, her party, her favourite food and where she went to school | How loud she talks, her clothes and her car | Who likes her, who hates her and who voted
exercise: Why did Liam's neighbours disagree about the swimming pools? | Some thought she lied, others that her plan was blocked | They all agreed she had built the pools but in the wrong place | None of them knew there had been an election | They were arguing about a different town
exercise: Two politicians said crime went up and crime went down. How could both use real numbers? | They chose different time periods | One of them made up every number | Crime numbers change every minute | The charts were drawn upside down
exercise: What did the politician do with the yes-or-no question? | Talked about something else instead | Answered «yes» very clearly | Answered «no» and explained why at length | Asked the reporter to repeat it
exercise: What was the «shocking secret» in the mayor headline? | He likes pineapple on pizza | He had stolen money from the town | He was secretly a famous singer | He had lied about the election
exercise: How did Leo's sister spot that the video was fake? | The mouth moved weirdly with the words | The video was in black and white | The politician wore a funny hat in it | The video was too short to be real
exercise: What did Leo's family check before believing the video? | Whether real news sites reported it | How many likes the video had | Whether the uncle was angry about it | Which colour the politician wore
exercise: Why could Max not keep his «no homework» promise? | A class captain has no power over homework | He forgot all about it the day after the vote | The teacher was on holiday | He changed his mind after winning
exercise: What question did Aisha's dad ask about the leaflet's promise? | Where will the money come from? | Who printed the leaflet, and where? | Why is the leaflet blue? | Is the candidate tall?
exercise: How was the politician's «I never said that!» checked? | A reporter played a recording of it | The politician swore on TV | His friends all said he didn't say it | Nobody could ever check it
exercise: What did Callum's mum suggest about the politician the neighbours argued over? | Look at what he said and did and decide | Believe the neighbour who shouts loudest | Always believe the honest-looking one | Never talk about politicians at all
exercise: What did Grace's teacher say about politicians? | Some lie, some don't, most do a mix | Every single one of them lies, every day | None of them ever lies at all | Only the ones on TV lie
exercise: Which is the most important skill the teacher named? | Checking | Shouting | Guessing | Voting
exercise: Is dodging a question always the same as lying? | No, but it can hide something | Yes, every single time, always | No, it is always completely honest | Yes, but only when it happens on TV
exercise: Why are headlines often bigger and louder than the story? | To make people read or click | To tell the whole truth quickly | Because the law asks for it | To save paper and ink
test: Uncle in Boston, wicked mad: «look what this guy said, sharing it!!» What should happen first? | Check if real news sites report it | Share it fast before somebody deletes it | Believe it because it's a video | Reply angrily under the post
test: Belfast neighbour: «Wise up, he's the only honest one.» What does «wise up» mean? | Stop being silly | Get cleverer at school | Stand up straight | Go off to sleep now
test: Kid in Sydney: «vote me captain, no homework ever, no cap». What's the problem? | A captain can't cancel homework | Nothing, he can easily do it | Captains are not allowed to say «no cap» | He must be the oldest kid
test: Dad in Toronto: «They're all liars, eh.» Which is the most accurate answer? | Some lie, some don't — we can check | Yes, every politician always lies | No, politicians never lie | Only foreign politicians lie
test: «Crime went DOWN!» says one. «Crime went UP!» says another. What question helps most? | Which years are you comparing? | Which of you is more famous? | Which one has nicer shoes? | Who spoke first?
test: What would happen if everyone believed every headline without reading the story? | Many people would believe things that aren't so | Everyone would know the full truth | Newspapers would stop having headlines on the front page | Nothing would change at all
test: Granny: «He talked for two minutes and never said yes or no.» What did she notice? | He dodged the question | He gave a very clear answer | He was a very slow speaker | He forgot what day it was
test: In a cartoon, the bad mayor twirls his moustache so everyone knows he's lying. What is real life more like? | You have to check what people said and did | Liars always have moustaches | Honest people always smile more than liars do | Liars always look at the floor
test: A leaflet: «Lower taxes AND more money for everything!» Aisha's dad: «Both? With what money?» What is he doing? | Asking how the promise would be paid for | Saying the candidate is a liar for sure | Saying the candidate is surely honest | Throwing the leaflet away unread
test: The pool wasn't built. Liam's mate: «she LIED, end of». Which is fairer? | Maybe — or maybe the plan was blocked; check | Yes, every broken promise is a lie | No, politicians can't break promises | It doesn't matter what she promised
test: Politician: «I never said that!» A reporter has a recording. What settles it best? | Playing the recording | Asking his best friend | Counting who believes him | Waiting a year or two to see
exam: Identify the principal reason for which two public figures may cite the same body of statistics in support of contrary conclusions. | Each selects the period or portion most favourable to his case | Statistics are invariably fabricated | Only one of them is able to read figures, the other guessing | Statistical data cannot be compared
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a video purporting to show a politician making a statement. | Its authenticity should be ascertained through reputable news sources before it is believed or shared | Its authenticity is guaranteed by the fact that it is a video, videos being impossible to alter by any means | It may be shared provided the viewer is angry | It constitutes conclusive proof of the statement
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an electoral promise that was subsequently not fulfilled? | It may represent a deliberate falsehood or a plan that failed, and the matter is frequently disputed | It invariably represents a deliberate falsehood, the politician having known from the outset that it could not be kept | It invariably represents an honest failure | It is not a matter that can be examined
exam: Determine which of the following constitutes the soundest approach to the assertion that all politicians are untruthful. | Examination of what each individual said and did | Acceptance of the assertion without reservation | Rejection of all criticism of politicians | Reliance upon the opinion expressed most loudly
